WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.720
This is the technical deep dive
on external partner identity,

3
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.376
from the HR-Driven Identity book of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

4
00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:13.024
and its Microsoft Entra profile
in Access and Privilege.

5
00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:16.829
It's for identity engineers,
Conditional Access owners,

6
00:00:16.829 --> 00:00:19.062
ISSOs and the governance office.

7
00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:23.537
After the rule the whole profile
enforces, it runs in three modules.

8
00:00:23.537 --> 00:00:27.476
Module 1 is assurance and the
default route into the tenant.

9
00:00:27.476 --> 00:00:32.738
Module 2 is cross-tenant synchronization
as an exception, and who does the work.

10
00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:37.382
Module 3 is proof: the partner
identity record, ten validation tests,

11
00:00:37.382 --> 00:00:42.524
monitoring, the requirements and
evidence, and the government-cloud check.

12
00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:47.800
Every Microsoft setting here comes from
Microsoft Learn, as the profile cites it.

13
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.500
Start with the rule the
whole profile enforces.

14
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.896
A partner account gets access
because a sponsor asked,

15
00:00:57.896 --> 00:01:02.725
an approver agreed and a date was set,
never because it exists or synchronized.

16
00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:05.178
The partner is authoritative
for the person's

17
00:01:05.178 --> 00:01:07.438
affiliation and the credential it issues.

18
00:01:07.438 --> 00:01:11.944
The agency is authoritative for
federation trust, eligibility,

19
00:01:11.944 --> 00:01:16.450
authorization, monitoring, review,
suspension and termination.

20
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.919
So engineering has four things
to prove for every partner:

21
00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:22.449
that trust is scoped
to that partner alone,

22
00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:26.279
that every account traces to an
approved request with an expiry,

23
00:01:26.279 --> 00:01:31.121
that every partner account is a guest
unless an exception record says otherwise,

24
00:01:31.121 --> 00:01:35.313
and that access really ends at the
last assignment and at partner exit.

25
00:01:35.313 --> 00:01:37.500
The rest of this deck is how.

26
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.112
Module 1 is assurance
and the default route.

27
00:01:44.112 --> 00:01:47.679
First, how much assurance each
partner transaction needs,

28
00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:49.313
and who performs each part.

29
00:01:49.313 --> 00:01:52.759
Then how a partner person gets
into the tenant the normal way:

30
00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:57.033
through a connected organization and
an access package, with cross-tenant

31
00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:01.730
trust scoped to that one partner, and
one partner list in Conditional Access.

32
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:15.267
NIST SP 800-63-4, final since July
31, 2025, separates three scales.

33
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:19.768
Identity proofing is usually done by
the partner, under its own practice.

34
00:02:19.768 --> 00:02:23.330
Authentication is done by the
partner's identity service.

35
00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:29.080
Federation assurance is set by the agency,
as relying party, for each application.

36
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:35.718
SP 800-63C-4 says what each
federation level demands.

37
00:02:35.718 --> 00:02:39.101
At level one, the identity
provider signs the assertion,

38
00:02:39.101 --> 00:02:42.779
and the relying party validates
it against the expected key,

39
00:02:42.779 --> 00:02:47.118
with the assertion audience-restricted
and protected against replay.

40
00:02:47.118 --> 00:02:51.955
Level two adds injection protection, a
transaction the relying party starts,

41
00:02:51.955 --> 00:02:57.094
an assertion for a single relying party,
a trust agreement established in advance,

42
00:02:57.094 --> 00:03:00.268
and identifiers without
plaintext personal data.

43
00:03:00.268 --> 00:03:03.967
Level three adds holder-of-key
or a bound authenticator.

44
00:03:03.967 --> 00:03:09.075
Selection follows NIST's steps: assess
impact per user group, take the initial

45
00:03:09.075 --> 00:03:14.581
levels, tailor, and document the result in
a Digital Identity Acceptance Statement.

46
00:03:14.581 --> 00:03:17.155
The chapter didn't read the October 6,

47
00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:25.340
2026 planning note on SP 800-63C-4
in full, so check it first.

48
00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.368
For partners outside the
agency's own organization,

49
00:03:32.368 --> 00:03:36.790
the route is entitlement management
with B2B collaboration guests.

50
00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.580
Create one connected organization per
partner, configured, never proposed:

51
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.978
a policy open to all users creates proposed
connected organizations automatically,

52
00:03:46.978 --> 00:03:51.539
so each partner policy targets
specific connected organizations only,

53
00:03:51.539 --> 00:03:53.440
and a proposed one is a finding.

54
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.003
Create one access package per partner
use case, in a catalog enabled

55
00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:02.403
for external users, hidden so only
people with the link can find it.

56
00:04:02.403 --> 00:04:06.990
Approval has two stages: the
agency sponsor, then the resource

57
00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:12.027
owner for the higher-risk applications,
with expiry at most 365 days.

58
00:04:12.027 --> 00:04:13.977
The sponsor reviews quarterly.

59
00:04:13.977 --> 00:04:16.478
When a guest loses the last assignment,

60
00:04:16.478 --> 00:04:20.228
sign-in is blocked and the
account is deleted after 30 days.

61
00:04:20.228 --> 00:04:22.428
And guests can't invite other guests.

62
00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:27.391
One caution from Microsoft Learn: Conditional
Access that affects guests can block

63
00:04:27.391 --> 00:04:32.290
My Access, so test that a partner can
still request before you add an exclusion.

64
00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.542
Every partner on Entra ID gets its own

65
00:04:38.542 --> 00:04:42.740
organization-specific entry in
cross-tenant access settings.

66
00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.220
Those settings override the defaults,

67
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.715
and MFA and device claims from other
tenants aren't trusted by default.

68
00:04:49.715 --> 00:04:53.983
Inbound B2B collaboration is
allowed for the partner tenant only,

69
00:04:53.983 --> 00:04:56.803
scoped to the access
package's applications.

70
00:04:56.803 --> 00:04:59.164
B2B direct connect is blocked.

71
00:04:59.164 --> 00:05:04.278
Trusting the partner's MFA is turned on
only where the agreement says the partner

72
00:05:04.278 --> 00:05:08.857
enforces it at home, and Conditional
Access still requires the strength;

73
00:05:08.857 --> 00:05:11.528
trusting MFA only skips the second prompt.

74
00:05:11.528 --> 00:05:14.700
Trust in compliant or
hybrid-joined devices stays off,

75
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.366
unless the agreement names the
partner's compliance standard.

76
00:05:18.366 --> 00:05:21.490
Automatic redemption stays
off on the default route.

77
00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:26.274
The record holds the partner's immutable
tenant ID and the names it resolves to,

78
00:05:26.274 --> 00:05:30.690
and a partner domain that resolves to
another tenant is a stop condition.

79
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:36.285
For a partner on SAML or WS-Fed federation,
Conditional Access can require only

80
00:05:36.285 --> 00:05:42.310
the MFA grant, so the agreement must say
whether the method is phishing-resistant.

81
00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.810
No new Conditional
Access policy is needed.

82
00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:53.585
Five policies in the workbook's library
already carry the partner tenant list.

83
00:05:53.585 --> 00:06:01.135
CA009, for MFA, and CA010, for
terms of use, include partner users.

84
00:06:01.135 --> 00:06:09.335
CA011, CA012 and CA038 apply guest limits
to everyone except the partner tenants.

85
00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:12.042
The list must be identical in all five;

86
00:06:12.042 --> 00:06:15.798
a partner in one and not the
others is a drift finding.

87
00:06:15.798 --> 00:06:18.213
The identity governance officer owns it,

88
00:06:18.213 --> 00:06:21.835
and a partner enters it only when
its access package goes live.

89
00:06:21.835 --> 00:06:24.460
The user type decides
which policies apply.

90
00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:29.741
CA011, CA012 and CA038 match
only guest-type external users,

91
00:06:29.741 --> 00:06:35.022
so an external member from an
unlisted tenant escapes them.

92
00:06:35.022 --> 00:06:38.360
A member also gets member-level
directory permissions,

93
00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:42.060
and falls outside persona rules
and reviews scoped to guests.

94
00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.735
That's why partner persons
are guests by default.

95
00:06:44.735 --> 00:06:48.589
A read-only Graph check compares
the list across the five policies

96
00:06:48.589 --> 00:06:52.740
and against the partner entries
in cross-tenant access settings.

97
00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:00.415
Module 2 is the exception:
cross-tenant synchronization.

98
00:07:00.415 --> 00:07:04.428
It covers the conditions that must
all hold before sync is allowed,

99
00:07:04.428 --> 00:07:08.004
the terms the agency states and
then tests in its own tenant,

100
00:07:08.004 --> 00:07:12.090
and how to end a sync relationship
without leaving accounts behind.

101
00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:17.090
It closes with who does the work, on
the partner's side and the agency's.

102
00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.315
Cross-tenant synchronization
pushes the partner's

103
00:07:24.315 --> 00:07:27.952
internal members into the
agency tenant as B2B users.

104
00:07:27.952 --> 00:07:32.775
Allow it only when every condition holds
and is written in the exception record:

105
00:07:32.775 --> 00:07:36.429
the partner's people are internal
members of an Entra tenant,

106
00:07:36.429 --> 00:07:40.741
the population is too large or changes
too fast for per-person requests,

107
00:07:40.741 --> 00:07:44.175
the agreement covers privacy,
consent and minimization,

108
00:07:44.175 --> 00:07:47.464
and the business owner decides,
with the sponsor named.

109
00:07:47.464 --> 00:07:49.851
The agency doesn't own the mappings,

110
00:07:49.851 --> 00:07:52.978
so it states them as terms
and tests the result.

111
00:07:52.978 --> 00:07:55.692
The user type mapping defaults to member;

112
00:07:55.692 --> 00:07:58.566
the profile requires
guest, applied always.

113
00:07:58.566 --> 00:08:03.028
Matching uses the alternative security
identifier and can't be changed.

114
00:08:03.028 --> 00:08:08.764
The deletion threshold defaults to 500,
so set it below the expected population.

115
00:08:08.764 --> 00:08:11.015
A failing job goes into quarantine.

116
00:08:11.015 --> 00:08:15.627
Scope only assigned users and
groups; nested groups don't cascade.

117
00:08:15.627 --> 00:08:21.140
And cycles run every 40 minutes, so
emergency suspension can't wait for one.

118
00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:25.940
Sync creates the account; access
still comes from an access package.

119
00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:31.765
Deprovisioning follows the source.

120
00:08:31.765 --> 00:08:34.769
When the partner deletes
or unassigns a user,

121
00:08:34.769 --> 00:08:39.802
the user is soft-deleted in the agency
tenant and can be restored for 30 days.

122
00:08:39.802 --> 00:08:43.346
A sign-in block set on the
agency side lasts only until the

123
00:08:43.346 --> 00:08:46.890
source user changes, and then
sync re-enables the account.

124
00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.439
So an agency suspension of a synced user

125
00:08:49.439 --> 00:08:52.452
also removes their access
package assignment.

126
00:08:52.452 --> 00:08:55.426
A severed relationship
leaves accounts behind:

127
00:08:55.426 --> 00:08:59.142
deleting the sync policy doesn't
remove the users it created,

128
00:08:59.142 --> 00:09:03.677
and turning off inbound sync has no
effect on users already synchronized.

129
00:09:03.677 --> 00:09:08.589
To end cleanly, the partner unassigns
everyone while the agency keeps

130
00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:13.416
inbound sync on, then the agency
turns it off and deletes what remains.

131
00:09:13.416 --> 00:09:18.753
On licensing, each synced user needs
Entra ID P1 in the source tenant.

132
00:09:18.753 --> 00:09:23.643
Cross-cloud sync carries users only,
and manager isn't synced across clouds,

133
00:09:23.643 --> 00:09:27.230
so no approval rule may
depend on a synced manager.

134
00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:34.655
The chapter's responsibility
table has one decider per row,

135
00:09:34.655 --> 00:09:37.955
the agency business owner,
and names who does the work.

136
00:09:37.955 --> 00:09:43.280
Identity engineering builds and runs the
credential checks, the attribute mappings,

137
00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.430
the authentication validation
and the lifecycle,

138
00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:48.980
including disables and reconciliation.

139
00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:51.463
The sponsor does the work on eligibility,

140
00:09:51.463 --> 00:09:55.042
authorization and the evidence,
and requests suspensions.

141
00:09:55.042 --> 00:10:00.018
Security operations contains incidents
first and tells the business owner after.

142
00:10:00.018 --> 00:10:03.364
The ISSO is consulted on
proofing, credentials,

143
00:10:03.364 --> 00:10:07.742
incidents and evidence, and the
privacy office on attributes.

144
00:10:07.742 --> 00:10:11.067
The identity governance
officer owns the partner tenant

145
00:10:11.067 --> 00:10:14.392
list and is alerted to any
change in cross-tenant settings.

146
00:10:14.392 --> 00:10:18.671
On the partner's side, the partner
keeps its affiliation record accurate,

147
00:10:18.671 --> 00:10:22.451
issues and revokes credentials,
sends mover and leaver notices,

148
00:10:22.451 --> 00:10:25.660
and coordinates incidents
under the agreement.

149
00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:31.260
Module 3 is proof.

150
00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:36.321
The partner identity record that holds
every decision, the ten validation tests,

151
00:10:36.321 --> 00:10:41.156
the monitoring and drift checks, the
requirements and evidence an ISSO keeps,

152
00:10:41.156 --> 00:10:43.649
and finally the government-cloud check,

153
00:10:43.649 --> 00:10:47.320
with the things the profile
honestly hasn't verified.

154
00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:53.806
Every partner relationship has one record,

155
00:10:53.806 --> 00:10:57.607
written before access is granted
and kept as evidence after.

156
00:10:57.607 --> 00:11:01.622
It lives in the repository beside
the configuration it describes,

157
00:11:01.622 --> 00:11:04.833
so a change to either arrives
in the same pull request.

158
00:11:04.833 --> 00:11:10.726
It has six sections: authority and purpose,
owners, risk, agreement and assurance,

159
00:11:10.726 --> 00:11:15.994
route and user type, lifecycle and
operations, and the readiness checks.

160
00:11:15.994 --> 00:11:18.357
Three rules apply to every field.

161
00:11:18.357 --> 00:11:22.529
Reference, don't copy: the
agreement, the privacy determination

162
00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:26.219
and the records schedule go
in as identifiers and dates.

163
00:11:26.219 --> 00:11:30.104
One decider: each readiness
check names the business owner,

164
00:11:30.104 --> 00:11:33.670
and the authorizing official
is consulted on the last.

165
00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:38.470
And a blank is a finding: write none
or not applicable, with a reason.

166
00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:43.639
The Entra profile adds the values the
record asks for: tenant relationship,

167
00:11:43.639 --> 00:11:47.223
cross-tenant settings,
configuration IDs, mappings,

168
00:11:47.223 --> 00:11:51.058
access governance, validation
results and monitoring.

169
00:11:51.058 --> 00:11:56.820
The record holds no personal data about
individual partner persons, and no secrets.

170
00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:05.770
The profile ships its validation tests as
copy-paste Microsoft Graph PowerShell.

171
00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:09.817
Run them in a test tenant with a test
partner tenant first; they were checked

172
00:12:09.817 --> 00:12:13.995
against the Graph PowerShell reference
but haven't been run against a tenant.

173
00:12:13.995 --> 00:12:17.607
Test zero confirms the tenant
ID resolves to the partner

174
00:12:17.607 --> 00:12:21.295
and that the trust and redemption
values match the record.

175
00:12:21.295 --> 00:12:25.903
Test one creates a guest through an
access package request, and checks

176
00:12:25.903 --> 00:12:30.670
the user type is guest and that My
Access still loads under the MFA policy.

177
00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:34.407
Test two finds any partner
account that isn't a guest.

178
00:12:34.407 --> 00:12:38.593
Three is emergency suspension,
four removes the assignment,

179
00:12:38.593 --> 00:12:42.191
five finds duplicates and
unredeemed invitations,

180
00:12:42.191 --> 00:12:47.296
six proves denial for a user with no
assignment and for an unlisted tenant,

181
00:12:47.296 --> 00:12:51.230
seven shows the MFA policy
applied, eight covers devices,

182
00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:53.908
nine rolls back, and ten is partner exit.

183
00:12:53.908 --> 00:13:00.390
Tests one, three, four, nine and ten
write, and each says how to undo it.

184
00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.050
Native sign-in and audit
retention in Entra is 30 days,

185
00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.627
so the logs stream to the workspace.

186
00:13:10.627 --> 00:13:12.102
Six signals matter.

187
00:13:12.102 --> 00:13:15.282
Provisioning logs for sync
live in the partner's tenant,

188
00:13:15.282 --> 00:13:17.402
so the agreement has to deliver them.

189
00:13:17.402 --> 00:13:20.208
On the agency side,
sync changes show up in

190
00:13:20.208 --> 00:13:23.014
the audit log under the sync fabric actor.

191
00:13:23.014 --> 00:13:28.190
Entitlement management records requests,
approvals, expiries and removals.

192
00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.853
The sign-in log shows the MFA policy's

193
00:13:30.853 --> 00:13:34.403
failures and any denied
attempts after removal.

194
00:13:34.403 --> 00:13:37.019
Cross-tenant settings
changes are audited and

195
00:13:37.019 --> 00:13:39.502
alerted to the identity
governance officer.

196
00:13:39.502 --> 00:13:44.071
And drift compares the repository with
the tenant: a partner list mismatch,

197
00:13:44.071 --> 00:13:48.565
any non-guest without a record, or trust
values that differ from the record.

198
00:13:48.565 --> 00:13:53.389
The profile suggests four alerts: a
member partner account with no record,

199
00:13:53.389 --> 00:13:57.896
a sync cycle deleting more than
expected, and a partner list mismatch,

200
00:13:57.896 --> 00:14:02.760
all high; and a cross-tenant change
made outside a pull request, medium.

201
00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:13.925
Each step maps to NIST SP 800-53 controls,
to FedRAMP Key Security Indicators

202
00:14:13.925 --> 00:14:18.560
where the workbook already pairs them,
and to the evidence the ISSO keeps.

203
00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:24.149
The partner entry and its trust
settings support AC-3, AC-20 and IA-8,

204
00:14:24.149 --> 00:14:30.360
with test zero's output and the
agreement clause behind each trust value.

205
00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:34.509
The access package, its approval
and expiry support AC-2,

206
00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:37.535
with the policy export and review history.

207
00:14:37.535 --> 00:14:41.723
Suspension, removal and exit
support automated account disabling,

208
00:14:41.723 --> 00:14:46.360
and stand in for the agency's own
personnel controls through the agreement.

209
00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:51.548
Denial and MFA tests
support IA-8 and IA-2.

210
00:14:51.548 --> 00:14:56.472
Monitoring supports AU-6,
and drift supports CA-7.

211
00:14:56.472 --> 00:15:00.992
Key Security Indicators describe
what cloud service providers report,

212
00:15:00.992 --> 00:15:05.360
so the agency doesn't report them; it
lines its own evidence up with them.

213
00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:09.649
And the cloud services involved
must sit inside a FedRAMP-certified

214
00:15:09.649 --> 00:15:13.480
offering that the agency's
own authorization relies on.

215
00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:21.147
Last, the government-cloud
check, read on November 6,

216
00:15:21.147 --> 00:15:24.505
2026 and rechecked
before each new partner.

217
00:15:24.505 --> 00:15:26.460
For an agency tenant in GCC,

218
00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:31.614
entitlement management and connected
organizations are available,

219
00:15:31.614 --> 00:15:36.591
and a partner in GCC High or DoD
connects as Entra ID in another cloud,

220
00:15:36.591 --> 00:15:39.968
with both tenants enabling
each other's cloud.

221
00:15:39.968 --> 00:15:45.048
B2B direct connect isn't supported across
clouds, and Teams doesn't support external

222
00:15:45.048 --> 00:15:50.056
members across clouds, so check each
application's government service description.

223
00:15:50.056 --> 00:15:52.542
Cross-cloud sync carries users only.

224
00:15:52.542 --> 00:15:55.604
Several things are honestly
marked not verified.

225
00:15:55.604 --> 00:15:58.948
Whether cross-cloud sync
is generally available:

